# COMBAT SPORTS OVERVIEW

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

This document defines the common characteristics shared by combat sports.

It provides the conceptual framework that allows the Combat Athlete System (CAS) to understand combat performance before specializing its reasoning for a specific discipline.

Every combat sport is unique.

However, they all share fundamental physical, physiological, biomechanical, technical and cognitive principles.

---

# Definition

A combat sport is a competitive activity in which two opponents interact directly while attempting to impose tactical, technical and physical superiority within a defined ruleset.

Victory may be achieved through:

Points.

Submission.

Knockout.

Technical superiority.

Referee decision.

Different rules produce different performance demands.

---

# Philosophy

Combat performance is never determined by a single quality.

Success emerges from the interaction between:

Physical capacity.

Technical skill.

Tactical intelligence.

Decision making.

Psychological resilience.

Recovery.

The role of CAS is to understand these interactions.

---

# Classification of Combat Sports

CAS distinguishes four major families.

---

## Striking Sports

Primary objective:

Hit the opponent.

Examples:

Boxing.

Kickboxing.

Muay Thai.

Savate.

Karate.

Taekwondo.

Primary characteristics:

Distance management.

Reaction speed.

Rotational power.

Footwork.

Precision.

Timing.

---

## Grappling Sports

Primary objective:

Control the opponent.

Examples:

Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu.

Wrestling.

Judo.

Sambo.

Primary characteristics:

Grip.

Leverage.

Positional dominance.

Relative strength.

Isometric endurance.

Body control.

---

## Hybrid Sports

Primary objective:

Integrate striking and grappling.

Examples:

Mixed Martial Arts.

Primary characteristics:

Rapid transitions.

Multi-range combat.

High technical complexity.

Large physiological demands.

---

## Self-Defense Systems

Primary objective:

Survive violent encounters.

Examples:

Krav Maga.

Military Combatives.

Law Enforcement Defensive Tactics.

Primary characteristics:

Unpredictability.

Environmental constraints.

Multiple attackers.

Stress management.

Decision making.

Legal considerations.

---

# Shared Performance Model

Despite their differences,

all combat sports require interaction between six domains.

Physical.

Technical.

Tactical.

Psychological.

Physiological.

Cognitive.

CAS considers all six simultaneously.

---

# Physical Qualities

The primary physical capabilities include:

Strength.

Power.

Speed.

Endurance.

Mobility.

Stability.

Recovery.

Energy System Development.

Their importance varies between disciplines.

---

# Technical Requirements

Technical performance includes:

Movement efficiency.

Skill execution.

Precision.

Timing.

Coordination.

Mechanical efficiency.

Technical quality must remain stable under fatigue.

---

# Tactical Requirements

Combat athletes continuously solve tactical problems.

Examples:

Distance management.

Rhythm control.

Opponent manipulation.

Feints.

Transitions.

Positioning.

Adaptation.

Decision quality often determines victory.

---

# Cognitive Requirements

Combat performance depends on:

Perception.

Attention.

Pattern recognition.

Reaction.

Anticipation.

Decision speed.

Working memory.

Situational awareness.

These abilities interact continuously.

---

# Psychological Requirements

Combat athletes require:

Confidence.

Emotional regulation.

Stress tolerance.

Self-control.

Aggressiveness under control.

Persistence.

Adaptability.

Psychological readiness influences physical expression.

---

# Physiological Requirements

Combat performance relies upon:

ATP-PC System.

Anaerobic Glycolysis.

Oxidative System.

Neuromuscular recovery.

Movement economy.

Repeated high-intensity efforts.

Every combat sport uses all three energy systems.

Only their relative contribution changes.

---

# Biomechanical Requirements

Combat movement depends upon:

Ground reaction force.

Force transmission.

Kinetic chains.

Rotation.

Stability.

Deceleration.

Impulse.

Effective mass.

Movement efficiency.

Biomechanics influences both performance and injury risk.

---

# Injury Characteristics

Common injury locations include:

Shoulder.

Neck.

Hand.

Wrist.

Knee.

Hip.

Ankle.

Lumbar spine.

Concussion risk exists in striking disciplines.

Joint injuries dominate many grappling disciplines.

Injury patterns differ between sports.

---

# Training Objectives

Combat training develops:

Technical skill.

Physical capacity.

Movement quality.

Recovery.

Decision making.

Sport-specific adaptations.

General physical preparation supports these objectives.

It never replaces technical practice.

---

# Transfer of Training

CAS evaluates transfer according to:

Movement similarity.

Force vector similarity.

Velocity similarity.

Range of motion.

Coordination.

Energy demands.

Decision demands.

Psychological demands.

Transfer should always be justified.

---

# Fatigue

Fatigue influences:

Technique.

Force production.

Speed.

Decision quality.

Coordination.

Movement variability.

Training should account for these interactions.

---

# Recovery

Recovery restores:

Neuromuscular readiness.

Metabolic function.

Movement quality.

Decision quality.

Psychological readiness.

Recovery is a performance variable.

---

# Assessment

CAS may assess:

Strength.

Power.

Speed.

Endurance.

Mobility.

Stability.

Movement quality.

Technical proficiency.

Recovery status.

Assessment depends on the athlete and the discipline.

---

# Individualization

No two athletes are identical.

Programming depends on:

Sport.

Competitive level.

Experience.

Age.

Training history.

Injury history.

Body morphology.

Goals.

Available equipment.

Recovery capacity.

CAS individualizes every recommendation.

---

# Explainability

